      ],
    })} />

    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'How dangerous are snakes in Australia really?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'The Australian Snakebite Project recorded 23 deaths from snakebite between July 2005 and June 2015, a median of two per year. Over the same period 1548 people were admitted with suspected snakebite, 835 of them were actually envenomed. 17 of the 23 deaths were attributed to brown snakes.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'How do snakebites actually happen in Australia?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'In 47.1 per cent of recorded cases the person was walking or doing something else, unknowingly near a snake. In 14 per cent they were trying to catch or kill it, and in 8.3 per cent it happened while gardening. The bite was to a lower limb in 52 per cent of cases.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Are there crocodiles in Byron Bay?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. On the east coast the saltwater crocodile occurs only as far south as roughly Gladstone and Rockhampton in Queensland. Byron Bay lies about a thousand kilometres further south in New South Wales, well outside that range.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'When can you see whales in Byron Bay?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Humpback whales migrate along the east coast between May and November. Cape Byron, the easternmost point of the Australian mainland, sits directly on their route. We saw our first whales on 3 July.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Are pythons in Australia dangerous?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Pythons are non-venomous. They kill prey not with venom but by wrapping around it and suffocating it. The species here are not life-threatening to people. A bite can still hurt and, like any bite wound, should be seen by a doctor. Keeping your distance and leaving the animal alone is entirely enough.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Should I worry more about animals in Australia with small children?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'We are here with two small children and have not had a single dangerous situation in three months, not even in the rainforest where snakes are part of the property. What changed are habits: closed shoes in the bush, stay on the path, carry a light at night, touch nothing. After two weeks you do it automatically.',
          },
        },
      ],
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <div slot="hero" class="hero-video">
    <InlineVideo
      src="/assets/images/au-buckelwal-loop.mp4"
      poster="/assets/images/au-buckelwal-drohne-byron.jpg"
      caption="26 July, off Byron Bay. I had gone out to film whales and spent half the day finding none."
      lang="en"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="toc-halter">
    <StickyToc eintraege={tocEintraege} lang="en" />
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <p class="info-box__titel">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In three months we have seen <strong>{gesehenAnzahl} species</strong> ourselves, from a dolphin on arrival day to a goanna in our own garden.</li>
      <li><strong>Three snakes</strong>, two of them since we moved into the rainforest. A green tree snake lives in the stone wall beside the pool. Not one encounter turned dangerous.</li>
      <li>According to the Australian Snakebite Project, a median of <strong>two people per year</strong> die of snakebite in Australia. Almost half of all bites happen because someone was near a snake without knowing.</li>
      <li><strong>There are no saltwater crocodiles or cassowaries here</strong>, and you will look for a wombat in vain on this stretch of coast. All three live somewhere else.</li>
      <li>At the bottom you will find a <strong>list to tick off and print</strong> for your own time here.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    “What about all those dangerous animals?” You hear that question a lot beforehand. Do you even see them in everyday life? And if you do, what is it actually like?
  </p>

  <p>
    I can answer it now, but not the way people expect. We <a href="/en/arrived-first-days-in-australia">arrived in Byron Bay on 25 June</a>. What has happened since then was less dramatic and considerably more beautiful than I had expected myself. And the one moment that genuinely mattered did not come from an animal at all. It came from a neighbour, and her story.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tally">Our tally after three months</h2>

  <p>
    This is not a list of animals that exist in Australia. It is the list of animals we have seen with our own eyes, with dates, on the east coast between Byron Bay and the hinterland. Every stamped field stands for a day we stood there and watched.
  </p>

  <figure class="pass">
    <figcaption class="pass__kopf">
      <span class="pass__titel">Our sightings pass</span>
      <span class="pass__zaehler">{gesehenAnzahl}<span class="pass__von">/{gesamt}</span></span>
    </figcaption>
    <div class="pass__gitter">
      {tiere.map((t) => (
        <div class={t.gesehen ? 'stempel stempel--voll' : 'stempel stempel--leer'}>
          <span class="stempel__name">{t.name}</span>
          {t.gesehen ? (
            <span class="stempel__datum">{new Date(t.gesehen).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: '2-digit', month: '2-digit' })}</span>
          ) : (
            <span class="stempel__offen">open</span>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="pass__fuss">
      Stamped means: seen ourselves. Not in a zoo, not on a tour, not on somebody else's phone.
    </p>
  </figure>

  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil={true} lang="en">
    Six fields are still empty. I hear the possum over the roof almost every night. I have never once seen it.
  </Randnotiz>

  <figure class="artikel-figur artikel-figur--alt">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-kaenguru-mit-joey-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-kaenguru-mit-joey-p960.jpg"
        width="720"
        height="960"
        alt="Kangaroo on a lawn with a joey looking out of the pouch, a shoulder blurred in the foreground."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Not from these three months.</strong> We have not seen a large kangaroo since 25 June. This picture is from January 2018, from our earlier time in Australia. It stands here for what we are still waiting for.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="first-days">The first encounters</h2>

  <p>
    The first interesting animal was not a snake, not a spider and nothing out of the sea. On arrival day a dark, chicken-sized bird was walking along the fence beside the coastal path, scratching in the leaf litter. No kangaroo, no koala. A brush turkey. I like them. But keep an eye on your food at the beach. I have watched them get a zip-lock bag open and tear into a packet of chips.
  </p>

  <p>
    Later the same day, from the beach, a dorsal fin in the water. Dolphins, on day one. I had not counted on that.
  </p>

  <p>
    The next evening, flying foxes hung upside down in a tree, the leaves still wet from the rain. Three days later a kookaburra sat on a sign pointing to the beach and let itself be looked at in peace.
  </p>

  <FotoStory
    lang="en"
    ueberschrift="The first week"
    bilder={[
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-buschhuhn-zaun-byron.jpg',
        alt: 'Brush turkey with a red head and yellow wattle scratching through leaf litter beside a fence post on the coastal path.',
        caption: '25 June: the very first animal. A brush turkey at the fence, not a kangaroo.',
      },
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-delfin-finne-tag-eins.jpg',
        alt: 'A dark dolphin back and dorsal fin breaking the grey surface of the sea, far from the beach.',
        caption: 'The same day, far out: one dorsal fin. That was all there was, and it was enough.',
      },
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-flughunde-baum-byron.jpg',
        alt: 'Several flying foxes hanging upside down with folded wings in the branches of a tree, rust-coloured fur around the neck.',
        caption: '26 June: flying foxes in the tree, the leaves still wet from the rain.',
      },
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-kookaburra-wegweiser.jpg',
        alt: 'Kookaburra sitting on a red sign reading Beach Access, green shrubs behind it.',
        caption: '28 June: the kookaburra stayed put until I was close enough.',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    On 30 June an echidna rummaged through the leaf litter at the foot of a rotting trunk, so close that I did not want to move. In a photograph an animal like that looks like a ball of spines. You only see the difference once it moves.
  </p>

  <InlineVideo
    src="/assets/images/au-echidna-loop.mp4"
    poster="/assets/images/au-echidna-loop-poster.jpg"
    caption="30 June, coastal bush near Byron Bay. Six seconds, no sound."
    lang="en"
    aspectRatio="9/16"
  />

  <PullQuote quelle="From the video on 4 July" lang="en">
    I set out to jog. It turned into more standing still than running.
  </PullQuote>

  <p>
    The fourth of July was the day both things happened. I ran up the Lighthouse Walk, and up ahead people were standing and looking upwards. There he sat, grey, in the fork of a branch. My first koala, after more than two years I had already spent in this country at an earlier time. A item on a list I did not know I was keeping.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-koala-eukalyptus-byron.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-koala-eukalyptus-byron.jpg"
        width="1200"
        height="1189"
        alt="A grey koala curled into the fork of a branch, its face with eye and ear visible between leaves."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>This is how you actually see koalas: half behind leaves, and only because somebody else stopped first.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Twenty minutes later, from the cliffs, a dark shape in the turquoise water. A shark, a big one, swimming in from the direction of the surfers. I let them know down below. Nobody seemed particularly bothered. I had been planning to surf myself that day and left it. My respect was a little larger than my plan.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-hai-unter-oberflaeche.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-hai-unter-oberflaeche.jpg"
        width="1200"
        height="1188"
        alt="Looking down onto turquoise water with a long dark shape just below the surface."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>That was all it was. And exactly why I stayed out of the water that day.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="whales">Whales, and how to miss them</h2>

  <p>
    Cape Byron is the easternmost point of the Australian mainland, which puts it directly on the humpback migration route. Between May and November they pass along the east coast, heading north and later back south again with their calves.
  </p>

  <figure class="chart-figur">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 300 74" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: humpback whales pass the east coast from May to November, with the highest values from July to October. From December to April no whales are expected.">
      <title>When humpback whales pass by</title>
      <text x="4" y="10" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#695A48">HUMPBACK WHALE — WHEN</text>
      {monate.map((m, i) => {
        const hoehe = [0, 0, 0, 0, 8, 18, 30, 32, 28, 26, 14, 0][i];
        const x = 12 + i * 23;
        return (
          <g>
            <rect x={x} y={58 - hoehe} width="15" height={hoehe} fill={hoehe > 20 ? '#B5482A' : '#7E8B6D'} />
            <text x={x + 7.5} y="70" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#695A48" text-anchor="middle">{m}</text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
    </svg>
    <figcaption>Based on figures from WWF Australia and Visit NSW. Terracotta marks the densest period.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    On 3 July I was standing at the housesit and they went past outside, far enough away that you could only make them out by the spouts. On 26 July I set off with the drone to finally get decent footage. First it was too windy. Then the whales were too far out. Then I found dolphins instead of whales.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-delfine-drohne-byron.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-delfine-drohne-byron.jpg"
        width="1200"
        height="675"
        alt="Aerial view of five dolphins just below the surface of green water, one lifting its tail fluke clear of the water."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>“No whales, but there are a few dolphins over there.” Five of them, one lifting its fluke.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    And then there were, closer than anything before. Later, when I was already back at the car, one swam past right next to the surfers at the bay where I usually surf. Earlier that same day I had filmed a koala. Some days hand you everything at once, after letting you walk for hours first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wallaby">The wallaby we had to wait for</h2>

  <p>
    On 3 July, the morning after my first night at the housesit, I said into the camera that wallabies were supposed to hop through this garden constantly. I had not seen a single one.
  </p>

  <p>
    It took seventeen days. On 20 July, on a dirt track through the coastal heathland south of Byron Bay, one stood in the middle of the path and looked back at me. Then another, and another. Four days later, one in the evening light, standing upright in the tall grass.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur artikel-figur--gross">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-wallaby-goldene-stunde-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-wallaby-goldene-stunde-p960.jpg"
        width="820"
        height="1093"
        alt="A wallaby standing upright in tall grass on a coastal dune, trees and the sea behind it in evening light."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>24 July, just before sunset. Seventeen days after I said there were supposedly wallabies here all the time.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <PullQuote lang="en">
    The waiting is part of it. It is the better part, in fact.
  </PullQuote>

  <p>
    That is exactly why this article ends with a list to tick off instead of a gallery. The appeal is not in having seen everything. It is in the empty fields.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then on 29 July something was sitting on a rock at the edge of the path, upright and completely motionless, and let me look at it from two metres away. Rust-red chest, a jagged crest along the neck, a dark stripe through the eye. From everything I can find, an eastern water dragon. I am not certain, and that is the bridge to the next part.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-wasseragame-stein-byron.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-wasseragame-stein-byron.jpg"
        width="1200"
        height="675"
        alt="A large lizard with a jagged neck crest, dark eye stripe and rust-red chest sitting upright among leaf litter and fern fronds."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>29 July. It did not move, and neither did I.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- ══════════ THE SERIOUS PART: different paper ══════════ -->
  <div class="ernst">
    <h2 id="snakes">Snakes: what the numbers say</h2>

    <p>
      On 23 July I was walking a shaded track through the coastal green and pushed my way through some scrub. Something small slid off the path into the grass in front of me. My first snake. To this day I do not know what it was. It was gone immediately, and there is no picture of it.
    </p>

    <div class="zeichnung-platz zeichnung-platz--ernst">
      <span class="zeichnung-platz__label">Drawing to come</span>
      <span class="zeichnung-platz__name">The snake at the edge of the path</span>
      <span class="zeichnung-platz__hinweis">Drawn by the kids, from my description. There is no photograph.</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      What occupied me more that day than the snake itself was a story somebody had told me shortly before.
    </p>

    <h3>The neighbour</h3>

    <p>
      A woman living at the housesit next door, <a href="/en/first-days-family-of-four-australia">where we spent our first weeks as a family</a>, helped look after the dogs. She has not been able to work for years, for health reasons. The cause goes a long way back: she was bitten by a brown snake, one of the most venomous species here.
    </p>

    <p>
      At the time she was living in a tipi with her children. She had to get up in the night, stood up, and in the dark she stepped on the snake right beside the mattress. Not in the bush. Not on a hike. Beside her own bed.
    </p>

    <p>
      On the property where I was staying at the time, she said, there lived one brown snake and several pythons. They generally stayed in the scrub, she said. Once she had nearly stepped on one again.
    </p>

    <Randnotiz ausrichtung="links" lang="en">
      “Very reassuring”, I thought at the time. And walked through that garden differently afterwards.
    </Randnotiz>

    <h3>So what do the numbers say?</h3>

    <p>
      In my video I said something like “maybe one death a year”. That was from memory. The solid source is the Australian Snakebite Project, a ten-year study run across Australian hospitals and published in the Medical Journal of Australia.
    </p>

    <div class="zahlen-block">
      <div class="zahlen-block__zeile">
        <span class="zahlen-block__zahl">23</span>
        <span class="zahlen-block__text">deaths from snakebite in ten years, July 2005 to June 2015. A median of <strong>two per year</strong>.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="zahlen-block__zeile">
        <span class="zahlen-block__zahl">17</span>
        <span class="zahlen-block__text">of those were attributed to <strong>brown snakes</strong>. Precisely the species that got my neighbour.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="zahlen-block__zeile">
        <span class="zahlen-block__zahl">1548</span>
        <span class="zahlen-block__text">people were admitted with suspected snakebite over the same period. 835 of them were actually envenomed.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      The part that surprised me most was not the death rate, though. It is the question of <em>how</em> people get bitten.
    </p>

    <figure class="chart-figur">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 300 108" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart on the circumstances of snakebites in Australia: in 47.1 per cent of cases the person was unknowingly near the snake, in 14 per cent they were trying to catch or kill it, and in 8.3 per cent it happened while gardening.">
        <title>How snakebites happen</title>
        <text x="4" y="10" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#695A48">HOW THE BITE HAPPENED (ASP, 2005–2015)</text>

        <text x="4" y="30" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#33291F">Unknowingly nearby</text>
        <rect x="4" y="34" width="235" height="12" fill="#B5482A" />
        <text x="245" y="43" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="9" fill="#A63E22">47.1 %</text>

        <text x="4" y="62" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#33291F">Trying to catch or kill it</text>
        <rect x="4" y="66" width="70" height="12" fill="#7E8B6D" />
        <text x="80" y="75" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="9" fill="#5C6850">14 %</text>

        <text x="4" y="94" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="8" fill="#33291F">While gardening</text>
        <rect x="4" y="98" width="42" height="8" fill="#7E8B6D" />
        <text x="52" y="106" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="9" fill="#5C6850">8.3 %</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>Australian Snakebite Project (ASP-20), Medical Journal of Australia 2017. 1548 recorded cases.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      So almost half of all bites happen not because a snake attacks somebody, but because somebody was walking along and noticed nothing. In 52 per cent of cases the bite was to a leg. And 31 per cent happened near houses, with a further 14 per cent <em>inside</em> buildings.
    </p>

    <p>
      Which makes my neighbour's story not an outlier. It makes it the standard case.
    </p>

    <h3>How to tell the dangerous ones apart</h3>

    <p>
      Honestly: reliably, you cannot. That is the most important sentence in this section. Even experienced people confuse species, and juveniles look different from adults. What you can learn is a rough distinction at a distance, nothing more.
    </p>

    <figure class="tafel">
      <figcaption class="tafel__kopf">Plate I — Rough distinction at a distance</figcaption>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" role="img" aria-label="Comparative drawing of two snakes. Above, a slender greenish snake with a thin body as an example of a non-venomous tree snake. Below, a solid single-coloured snake with an evenly thick body as an example of a brown snake. Arrows point to body thickness as the distinguishing feature.">
        <title>Slender tree snake compared with a solid brown snake</title>

        <path d="M20 46 q28-24 56 0 t56 0 t56 0" stroke="#7E8B6D" stroke-width="6" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <line x1="104" y1="18" x2="104" y2="32" stroke="#B5482A" stroke-width="1.5" />
        <path d="M100.5 30 l3.5 6 l3.5-6 z" fill="#B5482A" />
        <text x="112" y="26" font-family="Caveat, cursive" font-size="15" fill="#A63E22">thin, often greenish</text>
        <text x="20" y="70" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="9" fill="#5C6850">1. USUALLY HARMLESS — slender tree snake</text>

        <path d="M20 112 q28-24 56 0 t56 0 t56 0" stroke="#33291F" stroke-width="13" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <line x1="104" y1="80" x2="104" y2="94" stroke="#B5482A" stroke-width="1.5" />
        <path d="M100.5 92 l3.5 6 l3.5-6 z" fill="#B5482A" />
        <text x="112" y="88" font-family="Caveat, cursive" font-size="15" fill="#A63E22">solid, single colour</text>
        <text x="20" y="140" font-family="Courier Prime, monospace" font-size="9" fill="#A63E22">2. VENOMOUS — brown snake</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Schematic, not species-accurate. The arrows point to the one feature you can actually judge from a distance: body thickness.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <AufklappBox titel="What we taught the kids" lang="en">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Stay on the path.</strong> Not into tall grass, no reaching into leaf litter.</li>
        <li><strong>Closed shoes in the bush.</strong> More than half of all bites are to the leg.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop instead of running.</strong> If there is a snake: halt, keep your distance, let it move away.</li>
        <li><strong>Never touch, never shoo it off.</strong> Not even with a stick.</li>
        <li><strong>Take a light at night.</strong> The neighbour's story happened in the dark, two steps from her bed.</li>
      </ul>
    </AufklappBox>

    <div class="hinweis-medizin">
      <p>
        <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If a snakebite is suspected in Australia, call <strong>000</strong> immediately. The official first aid guidance is a pressure bandage with immobilisation, do not let the person walk, do not wash the wound and do not attempt to suck out venom. The current instructions are published by <a href="https://www.healthdirect.gov.au/snake-bites" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">healthdirect Australia</a>. We only pass on what we have learned ourselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- ══════════ END OF THE SERIOUS PART ══════════ -->

  <h2 id="rainforest">Postscript: the rainforest</h2>

  <p>
    Everything up to here happened on the coast, during the first two months. At the end of August we moved, away from the ocean and into the hinterland. A property in the rainforest, pool, horse paddock, open veranda. I said at the time that everything was a bit more basic here. That is true. What I had not seen coming: the list that had grown steadily over two months almost fell over itself in the two weeks that followed.
  </p>

  <h3>The last days at the old address</h3>

  <p>
    It started small. On 21 August a small brown lizard sat on the brick wall next to the cushion on the veranda, early in the morning. Four days later a cane toad sat on the same concrete, fist-sized, entirely unimpressed. I carefully nudged it towards the grass with the broom handle. The kids watched intently, the way they always do.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-cane-toad-besen-veranda.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-cane-toad-besen-veranda.jpg"
        width="1400"
        height="788"
        alt="On a concrete veranda someone in Ugg boots pushes a small dark cane toad towards the edge with a wooden broom, glass fence and rainforest slope behind."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>25 August, on the veranda. The cane toad just before it disappears back into the grass. A broom handle is all the equipment this takes.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The next day a huntsman sat on the pool net at the edge of the water. Palm-sized, flat, motionless. That evening a second cane toad in the grass by the roadside. I had gone six weeks without seeing any of them. Then three in two days.
  </p>

  <h3>Moving day already had a visitor</h3>

  <p>
    On 28 August we filled the car twice, an hour each way. Just after seven in the evening a gecko was sitting on the dark ceiling beam. Twenty minutes later it was on the fringed blanket on the sofa. First evening, first animal in the house, and honestly it was more funny than anything else.
  </p>

  <h3>3 September</h3>

  <p>
    Just after three in the afternoon something thin and green-brown moved through the grass at the foot of the stone wall and disappeared into the ferns beside the pool. We stood there and waited. A few minutes later it came back out across the red terrace tiles, entirely unhurried, and for the first time ever I had a snake on video. A green tree snake. Non-venomous.
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-green-tree-snake-terrasse.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-green-tree-snake-terrasse.jpg"
        width="1200"
        height="675"
        alt="Slender olive-green tree snake stretched along the foot of a natural stone wall on reddish terrace tiles, ferns growing out of the wall."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>3 September, just after 3pm. The first snake we did not just glimpse but actually stood and watched.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    That same night I almost stepped on a python. I did not notice a thing. I was walking along the path in the dark with Linnea on my arm, and behind me Lucy gave a start and called out "python". With the torch we then saw it, right beside the gravel path in front of a bush.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pythons are harmless. Even so, it was a sign: in the dark especially, it pays to look at your surroundings properly.
  </p>

  <h3>And then it simply kept going</h3>

  <p>
    On 5 September Joris stood at the open door in the morning, pointing outside, excited. A goanna, down in the sloping garden. By the time I got there with the camera, only the bush was still moving. Counts as seen, I would argue. So, ticked off.
  </p>

  <p>
    On 12 September, at night, a large flat spider on the white outside wall, lit by the lamp. On 13 September a small lizard ran across the breakfast table in the morning and stopped in the strip of sunlight between the peanut butter and the storage jars. You get used to it faster than you would think.
  </p>

  <FotoStory
    lang="en"
    ueberschrift="The small housemates"
    bilder={[
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-huntsman-poolkescher.jpg',
        alt: 'Large brown huntsman spider with outstretched legs sits on a turquoise plastic pool net above murky water.',
        caption: '26 August: the huntsman was sitting on the pool net. The pool got cleaned that day anyway.',
      },
      {
        src: '/assets/images/au-gecko-sofadecke.jpg',
        alt: 'Small dark gecko sitting on a cream-coloured fringed blanket on a sofa.',
