  />

  <h3>14 September, two snakes in one day</h3>

  <p>
    At 9:54 in the morning it was back. Same wall, same corner by the pool as eleven days earlier. A green tree snake, olive-bronze back, pale salmon flank, and we watched it for ten minutes straight as it pulled itself between the boulders and slid back under the leaves.
  </p>

  <InlineVideo
    src="/assets/images/au-green-tree-snake-loop.mp4"
    poster="/assets/images/au-green-tree-snake-loop-poster.jpg"
    caption="14 September, just after ten. Five seconds out of ten minutes of watching, no sound."
    lang="en"
    aspectRatio="16 / 9"
  />

  <p>
    That is the point where something shifted for me. It is no longer an encounter. I have seen it several times now in exactly that spot. <strong>The snakes live there.</strong> They are not an event, they are neighbours. And you settle in differently once you have understood that, compared to waiting for the one dramatic moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    At ten to four that afternoon, about ninety metres from the house, the second one was lying there. Thick, powerful, a high-contrast black and cream pattern, coiled in a crack in the rock. A python. And then came the question this whole article is really building towards, from one of the children:
  </p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur artikel-figur--alt">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-python-felsspalte.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/au-python-felsspalte.jpg"
        width="1080"
        height="1920"
        alt="Powerful python with a high-contrast black and cream pattern lies coiled between two lichen-covered rocks surrounded by green."
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Same hour, same property.</strong> The difference in build compared to the green tree snake that morning is why you never mix the two up. And why the question came.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <PullQuote quelle="14 September, from the video" lang="en">
    Are they not dangerous at all?
  </PullQuote>

  <p>
    The answer was: they are not venomous. No, they are not. And the answer is correct. Pythons kill by constriction, not venom. For a human the ones here are not a problem. For a chicken they are.
  </p>

  <h3>Lucy's side of it</h3>

  <p>
    I am the one with the camera, which is why this is my version. Lucy has her own, and hers is more matter-of-fact. She was out with Linnea when a very large python was lying on the road a few minutes from our future house. Her explanation was neither bad luck nor drama, it was geography: there is a river here, there is rainforest here, so there are more snakes here. Plus big cane toads and, as she puts it, properly green frogs.
  </p>

  <p>
    The house we are moving into next has no flyscreens on the doors and windows. Lucy wants them fitted, and her reasoning is the best sentence either of us has produced on this subject:
  </p>

  <PullQuote quelle="Lucy, late August" lang="en">
    Because I do want to know what is inside the house, and what I can expect when I walk around somewhere and clear something out.
  </PullQuote>

  <p>
    I think we have to find the right balance here. Treat the new situation with respect and still try to let go of the fear. It will certainly take a few more months before you get used to it. We are looking forward to every single day.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-here">What you will not find here</h2>

  <p>
    A large share of the fear of Australia is aimed at animals that simply do not live in our corner of it. That belongs in an honest answer.
  </p>

  <figure class="nichtda">
    <figcaption>Does not occur here</figcaption>
    <div class="nichtda__reihe">
      <div class="zeichnung-platz zeichnung-platz--durchgestrichen">
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__label">Drawing to come</span>
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__name">Saltwater Crocodile</span>
      </div>
      <div class="zeichnung-platz zeichnung-platz--durchgestrichen">
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__label">Drawing to come</span>
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__name">Cassowary</span>
      </div>
      <div class="zeichnung-platz zeichnung-platz--durchgestrichen">
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__label">Drawing to come</span>
        <span class="zeichnung-platz__name">Wombat</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="nichtda__text">
      On the east coast the <strong>saltwater crocodile</strong> occurs only as far south as roughly Gladstone and Rockhampton in Queensland. We are about a thousand kilometres further south. The <strong>cassowary</strong> lives in the Wet Tropics of north-eastern Queensland, between Cooktown and Townsville. Both are as far from here as Hamburg is from Rome.
    </p>
    <p class="nichtda__text">
      The <strong>wombat</strong> surprised me. It sat on our list for a long time, until I looked it up. According to the <a href="https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/bare-nosed-wombat/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Australian Museum</a>, in northern New South Wales it lives only in sclerophyll forest above about 600 metres, and it avoids rainforest. We live in rainforest, considerably lower. It dropped off our list before we ever got to tick it.
    </p>
  </figure>

