  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <AufklappBox titel="How dangerous are snakes in Australia really?" lang="en">
    <p>
      Between July 2005 and June 2015 the Australian Snakebite Project recorded 23 deaths, a median of two per year. Over the same period 1548 people were admitted with suspected snakebite, 835 of them actually envenomed. 17 of the 23 deaths were attributed to brown snakes.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <AufklappBox titel="How do bites happen in the first place?" lang="en">
    <p>
      In 47.1 per cent of cases the person was walking or doing something else, unknowingly near the snake. In 14 per cent they were trying to catch or kill it, and in 8.3 per cent it happened while gardening. In 52 per cent the bite was to a leg. Almost half of all cases happened in or immediately around buildings.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <AufklappBox titel="Are there crocodiles in Byron Bay?" lang="en">
    <p>
      No. On the east coast the saltwater crocodile occurs only as far south as roughly Gladstone and Rockhampton in Queensland, about a thousand kilometres north of here.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <AufklappBox titel="When can you see whales here?" lang="en">
    <p>
      Humpback whales pass along the east coast between May and November. Cape Byron, the easternmost point of the Australian mainland, sits directly on the route. We saw our first on 3 July and the best one on 26 July, right beside the surfers.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <AufklappBox titel="Are pythons in Australia dangerous?" lang="en">
    <p>
      Pythons are non-venomous. They kill prey not with venom but by wrapping around it and suffocating it, as the <a href="https://australian.museum/learn/animals/reptiles/diamond-python/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Australian Museum</a> describes it. The species here are not life-threatening to people. A bite can still hurt and, like any bite wound, should be seen by a doctor. Keeping your distance and leaving the animal alone is entirely enough.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <AufklappBox titel="Should I worry more with small children?" lang="en">
    <p>
      We are here with two small children and have not had a single dangerous situation in three months, not even since we moved into the rainforest and snakes became part of the property. What has changed are habits: closed shoes in the bush, stay on the path, carry a light at night, touch nothing. It is less effort than it sounds, and after two weeks you do it automatically. We have still had one proper fright. What we talked through with the children before moving is in our <a href="/en/moving-to-australia-with-kids-family-guide">guide to moving with kids</a>.
    </p>
  </AufklappBox>

  <div class="trust-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Where these details come from:</strong> every sighting in this article is our own observation between 25 June and 14 September 2026, first around Byron Bay and since late August in the hinterland. Every single one is backed by our own video and photo material with recording dates. The snakebite figures come from the
      <a href="https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2017/207/3/australian-snakebite-project-2005-2015-asp-20" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Australian Snakebite Project (ASP-20)</a>,
      Medical Journal of Australia 2017. Whale season according to WWF Australia and Visit NSW, the wombat range according to the
      <a href="https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/bare-nosed-wombat/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Australian Museum</a>,
      saltwater crocodile and cassowary ranges according to Australian government agencies and conservation organisations.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-log">
    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> 17 September 2026. Published on 3 September, extended since then by the rainforest section, two snake species, goanna, gecko and cane toad. The list keeps growing and we keep updating it.</p>
  </div>

